{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"6f3a0439e4b87fad","name":"card_event_form_battle_line","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/OceaniaStorm/events/card_event_form_battle_line","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"FORM BATTLE LINE","text":"Choose One - Add three LIGHT INFANTRY to your support line with Blitz OR +1+1.","flavorText":"From 1943, the Red Army rarely fought from a single neat line. Its battle positions were usually built in depth, with forward troops, supporting weapons, reserves, and command posts spread across several layers. Rifle companies held trenches, foxholes, villages, woods, or reverse slopes. Behind them stood mortars, machine guns, anti tank rifles, field guns, and reserve groups ready to seal gaps or counterattack. As their battle experience grew, Soviet commanders became better at arranging these layers into a connected system. Engineers laid mines and built obstacles along likely routes of attack. Artillery was grouped to support the front lines and strike enemy assembly areas. Anti-tank strongpoints covered roads, open ground, and approaches to bridges. Tanks were not only used for attacks, but also kept in reserve to meet enemy armor that had broken through. This method reflected the hard lessons from 1941 and 1942, when thin defenses were often shattered by rapid German attacks. By later campaigns, Soviet battle lines were meant to slow the enemy, absorb pressure, and seize opportunities for a counterstrike.","faction":"Soviet","type":"order","rarity":"Uncommon","cost":4,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_form_battle_line","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_form_battle_line_title","SourceString":"FORM BATTLE LINE","LocalizedString":"FORM BATTLE LINE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":4,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_form_battle_line_text","SourceString":"Choose One - Add three